From: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] libretro: VDP rendering and emulation on their own threads

The emulation thread ran both SH2s and then drew the frame (VDP1
commands, VDP2 layers, the GL calls): about 45 % of its time in
Virtua Fighter 2, which ran at 28 fps on a Cortex-A53 at 1.4 GHz.

- The asynchronous renderer upstream has for its Android and SDL ports
  (YAB_ASYNC_RENDERING: VdpProc draws VDP1/VDP2 while the SH2s run) is
  built for the libretro core.
- Frame N is drawn while the SH2s run frame N+1 (one frame of latency
  more): the VDP thread has an EGL context of its own sharing
  RetroArch's objects and composes each frame into one of two textures
  (YuiSwapBuffers publishes it with a fence); retro_run copies the
  newest into RetroArch's framebuffer and fences that copy before the
  texture is reused. The renderer reads the VDP2 registers and line
  tables through nuub_vdp2view.h from a copy taken at VBlank-IN, so the
  SH2s can change them meanwhile.
- The SH2s run on a thread of their own (emu_main, with a third context
  for their reads of the VDP1 framebuffer) while the main thread
  presents the previous frame; whatever touches the emulation from the
  main thread (options, states, cheats, reset, unload, context loss)
  waits for the running frame first.
- The VDP1 draw-end sync no longer stops the SH2s while the VDP thread
  is still composing the previous frame: they look again 10 lines
  later, as the synchronous renderer does while VDP1 is busy, and wait
  only before VBlank-IN.

Virtua Fighter 2 fight: 28 -> ~57 fps (with 0006 and 0007).

Upstream-Status: Pending
